Subject: [PATCH] yttrium: bound in-flight upload buffers in 32-bit processes

Upload buffers (u_upload_mgr buffers under the ordered context worker)
are mapped blobs, and each stays alive until the last batch that used
it retires. Batches are retired by polling when a new one is acquired,
up to 64 wait to be submitted as one group, and the pool grows to 256
batches. A program that streams uploads faster than the host completes
them therefore keeps hundreds alive. Unigine Valley's D3D9 renderer, a
32-bit process, creates about 450 MB/s of 4 MiB upload buffers; it held
over 1 GiB of upload maps, ran out of its 4 GiB address space, and hung
or crashed (compat finding F6).

Count the bytes of live upload buffers. After each context flush, while
they exceed the budget, submit the pending group, retire the completed
batches, then wait for the oldest batch. The budget is 256 MiB in a
32-bit process and unbounded in a 64-bit one; UPLOAD_BUDGET_MB in
yttrium.ini overrides it (0 turns it off).

With UPLOAD_STATS=1 (yttrium.ini or D3D10UMD_YTTRIUM_UPLOAD_STATS in the
environment), log every 5 s the created and live counts and bytes of
upload buffers, other buffers, textures, buffer-replacement storages,
the winsys's own Venus memory mappings, blob-mapped and heap-backed
resources. Off by default.

Evidence: docs/f6va.md, docs/evidence/f6va/.
Origin: vmlab-win3d f6va worker.
